+# Age-Restricted Products Design
+
+## Purpose and scope
+
+Saleor must let staff mark products as age-restricted and prevent ineligible
+customers from completing purchases. Restricted products remain visible in every
+catalog context; availability is not changed. The storefront must display the
+store's current age threshold as a label such as `18+`.
+
+The restriction applies to:
+
+- customer `checkoutComplete` requests;
+- staff `draftOrderComplete` requests; and
+- any product line in either flow that belongs to an age-restricted product.
+
+`orderBulkCreate` remains exempt because it imports historical orders.
+
+The setting is store-wide, has no channel or regional variation, and defaults to
+18. Changing it affects all later eligibility checks. It does not alter already
+completed orders.
+
+## Data model
+
+Add three database-backed fields:
+
+| Model | Field | Type and default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `Product` | `is_age_restricted` | Boolean, `False` | Whether the product requires an age check to buy. |
+| `User` | `birthday` | Nullable date | The customer's self-reported date of birth. |
+| `SiteSettings` | `minimum_age` | Positive integer, `18` | The single age threshold used by all age-restricted products. |
+
+Database migrations add the fields without modifying existing products,
+customers, or site settings. Existing products are unrestricted and existing
+customers have no birthday, so only a later attempt to buy a restricted product
+is affected.
+
+`minimum_age` accepts positive whole years. `birthday` is date-only, may be
+cleared, and rejects future dates.
+
+## API and authorization
+
+Expose `isAgeRestricted` on `Product`, `birthday` on `User`, and `minimumAge`
+on the shop/site-settings GraphQL surfaces. Add the corresponding typed inputs
+to product, account/customer, and site-settings mutations.
+
+Authorization is intentionally narrower than the normal customer-management
+permission:
+
+- A customer may set, change, or clear only their own birthday through
+  `accountUpdate`.
+- A superuser may set, change, or clear a customer's birthday through the
+  staff customer mutation.
+- Ordinary staff and apps cannot set or change a birthday, even when they have
+  `MANAGE_USERS` or impersonation permissions.
+- Product managers may set `isAgeRestricted` through the existing product
+  create/update authorization.
+- The existing site-settings permission controls `minimumAge`.
+
+The backend validates this at mutation level; hiding fields in a client is not
+an authorization boundary.
+
+## Birthday privacy and audit trail
+
+Birthday is personal data. It is readable only by the customer who owns the
+account and by a superuser. The GraphQL resolver must return it only in those
+contexts; ordinary staff, apps, unauthenticated callers, and other customers
+cannot retrieve it.
+
+Birthday must not appear in public product/customer APIs, public profiles,
+unauthenticated endpoints, webhook payloads, or exports. Dashboard support and
+sales users receive no birthday value. If a future staff workflow needs an
+eligibility signal, it may expose only a yes/no age-eligible result and must
+not reveal the birthday or derived age.
+
+Every birthday create, change, or clear operation is audit-logged with the
+actor, target customer, old/new state as permitted by the audit system, and
+timestamp. Every superuser read is audit-logged with the actor, target
+customer, and timestamp. Internal eligibility checks are not staff reads and
+do not create read-audit events.
+
+## Eligibility service
+
+Introduce one domain-level eligibility service shared by checkout and draft
+completion. It receives the order/check-out lines, the linked customer if any,
+the current `SiteSettings.minimum_age`, and the current date in Saleor's
+configured Django `TIME_ZONE`, which is the shop time zone for this feature.
+
+The service:
+
+1. Returns successfully when no line has an age-restricted product.
+2. Returns successfully for an unlinked draft order, as staff may complete it
+   without a customer.
+3. Rejects a customer checkout with no authenticated customer.
+4. Rejects a linked customer whose birthday is absent, in the future, or does
+   not reach the current minimum age.
+5. Accepts the customer when their birthday anniversary is on or before the
+   shop-local current date.
+
+Age is calculated using dates, not timestamps. A customer is eligible on their
+birthday. For a 29 February birthday, use 1 March as the anniversary in a
+non-leap year.
+
+The service must load product restriction state efficiently for all lines and
+must not use product visibility or catalog availability as an eligibility
+proxy.
+
+## Completion integration and errors
+
+Call the shared service in `checkoutComplete` after resolving the checkout,
+its lines, linked customer, and site settings, but before payment, stock,
+order-creation, or notification side effects.
+
+Call the same service in `draftOrderComplete` after draft validation and before
+the state transition, allocation, or notification side effects. An unlinked
+draft is permitted; a linked customer without a valid birthday is rejected
+until staff removes the customer link or the customer/superuser supplies a
+birthday.
+
+Add dedicated checkout and order error codes for an unmet age restriction. The
+error must be safe to display and must not expose the birthday or computed age.
+The storefront distinguishes a guest from an authenticated customer from its
+session state: a guest receives the age-restriction error and is directed to
+sign in with their cart preserved; an authenticated customer is directed to
+update their profile.
+
+No age validation is added to `orderBulkCreate`.
+
+## Dashboard and storefront
+
+Dashboard changes:
+
+- Add an "Age-restricted product" boolean control to product create/update.
+- Add the minimum-age integer control to site settings, with validation and the
+  existing site-settings permission.
+- Do not show customer birthday controls to ordinary staff. Show them only to
+  a superuser, with the API continuing to enforce that distinction.
+
+Storefront changes:
+
+- Extend product/catalog GraphQL fragments with `isAgeRestricted`. Fetch the
+  store's `minimumAge` from the `Shop` query and provide it to all catalog,
+  product-detail, cart, and checkout label renderers.
+- Render the current threshold as an age label (for example, `18+`) wherever a
+  restricted product is presented, including catalog/search cards, product
+  detail, and cart/checkout line contexts. The label is informational and must
+  not hide or disable browsing.
+- Add a date-only birthday input to the authenticated account profile using
+  `accountUpdate`.
+- On a guest age-restriction completion error, preserve the cart and redirect
+  to sign-in. On an authenticated error, preserve the cart and direct the
+  customer to their profile.
+
+## Testing
+
+Backend tests cover:
+
+- model migrations and defaults;
+- GraphQL field/input exposure and validation;
+- birthday write authorization for self, superuser, ordinary staff, and app;
+- birthday read authorization for self and superuser, with denials for
+  ordinary staff, apps, anonymous callers, and other customers;
+- no birthday in public APIs, webhook payloads, or export payloads;
+- audit entries for every birthday mutation and superuser read;
+- product and site-setting mutation permissions;
+- eligibility service cases for unrestricted lines, guest checkouts, linked
+  draft orders, absent/future/underage birthdays, the birthday boundary, and
+  29 February in leap and non-leap years;
+- rejected checkout and draft completion before payment, allocation, or order
+  side effects; and
+- `orderBulkCreate` remaining unaffected.
+
+Dashboard tests cover product and site-setting form submission/validation, plus
+the superuser-only customer-birthday control. Storefront tests cover age-label
+rendering, profile birthday submission, and cart-preserving guest/authenticated
+error handling.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+This feature does not verify identity, restrict catalog visibility, introduce
+regional/channel thresholds, use product attributes or metadata as the policy
+source, or re-evaluate completed historical orders.
